 [F2] The complex sine and cosine are entire and have derivatives $s_{\mathbb C}'(z)=c_{\mathbb C}(z)$ and $c_{\mathbb C}'(z)=-s_{\mathbb C}(z)$ ([[cor-complex-trigonometric-and-hyperbolic-derivatives]]).
 
-[F3] If $f=u+iv$ is complex differentiable at $z=x+iy$, then its real total derivative exists, $u_x(x,y)=\operatorname{Re}(f'(z))$, and $v_x(x,y)=\operatorname{Im}(f'(z))$ ([[thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann]]).
+[F3] Let $U\subseteq\mathbb C$ be open, let $z=x+iy\in U$, and let $f=u+iv:U\to\mathbb C$. If $f$ is complex differentiable at $z$, then its real total derivative exists, $u_x(x,y)=\operatorname{Re}(f'(z))$, and $v_x(x,y)=\operatorname{Im}(f'(z))$ ([[thm-complex-differentiability-real-linearity-wirtinger-and-cauchy-riemann]]). In both applications below, $U=\mathbb C$ and $z=t$ by the entire-function hypothesis in [F2].
 
